/Pg 14 0 R
/K [18]
>>
endobj
216 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 44 0 R
/E (For the Enterprise Gym Membership Management System, a low-code platform can be used effectively to develop the standard parts of the system rapidly: forms for membership registration, member and plan details, reports on attendance and membership history, workflows for request processing and approval, notifications, and basic dashboards. These features correspond to the capabilities that low-code platforms are designed to provide.)
/Pg 14 0 R
/K [19]
>>
endobj
217 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 44 0 R
/E (As the organisation grows, the requirements become more demanding. A large number of members, branches and attendance records raises performance and data-management concerns; integration with payment, CRM, inventory and access-control systems raises integration concerns; differing access needs and the handling of member data raise security and governance concerns; and advanced features such as intelligent analysis may need external services. In such cases, extensions, integrations or custom development may be needed in addition to the low-code application. Low-code is therefore useful for rapidly developing standard business applications, whereas increasingly complex enterprise requirements may call for a hybrid approach, and the choice should be based on the requirements of the application.)
/Pg 14 0 R
/K [20]
>>
endobj
218 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 44 0 R
/E (Experiment 13)
/Pg 14 0 R
/K [21]
>>
endobj
