394 0 obj
<<
/Title (Low-code platforms allow applications to be developed mainly through visual development tools instead of writing large amounts of traditional program code. Applications are assembled using predefined components such as forms, reports and dashboards, and behaviour is defined through workflows, rules and configuration. A small amount of scripting is usually available for specific needs. This approach reduces development time and effort and makes it possible for a wider range of people to take part in building business applications.)
/Dest [10 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 349 0 R
/Next 395 0 R
>>
endobj
395 0 obj
<<
/Title (Low-code platforms are well suited to standard business applications. As the requirements of an enterprise grow in terms of users, data volume, process complexity, integrations, security and customisation, the built-in capabilities of a platform may no longer be sufficient on their own. This case study examines such limitations through an enterprise gym membership scenario.)
/Dest [10 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 349 0 R
/Prev 394 0 R
>>
endobj
396 0 obj
<<
/Title (The architecture of this system on a low-code platform is arranged as six groups that are connected from left to right:)
/Dest [10 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 313 0 R
/Prev 351 0 R
/Next 435 0 R
>>
endobj
397 0 obj
<<
/Title (The groups interact in sequence. Users enter and view information through the application; the business logic and workflow layer processes this information; the data layer stores and supplies it; external systems provide or receive related information such as payments, customer relationships and equipment inventory; and the analytics layer reports on the results.)
/Dest [11 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 313 0 R
/Prev 436 0 R
/Next 352 0 R
>>
endobj
